Ouch.  I've been playing since the 80's as well (Rogue back then,
nethack in the '92 range or so) and didn't ascend until this year. 
Now I've done so 4 times.  I think the key for me was simply to be
patient.



Don't read any unidentified scroll or quaff any unknown potion.

You can put on non-cursed rings, but take them off immediately if they
don't auto-id.

Don't use an item (scrolls especially) that's uncursed and would be
better off blessed.

When you find an altar of your own alignment, take advantage of it for
a LONG time.

Leave locked chests alone until you find a way to open them without
breaking the contents.



Basically, the early game is about patience in the sense of being
willing to NOT have many cool goodies to use until you've gotten some
blessed identifies to use and prioritized what to ID appropriately.



Even after that, patience and care are still paramount.



If you run into a tough monster, be willing to retreat and hang out on
lower levels until you're able to dispatch it safely--don't go into
any battle that is even CLOSE to even odds.  If you're going to have a
thousand fights on the way to ascension, you need to have better than
999:1 odds on average to avoid death.

Be devastatingly overprepared before attempting the quest, or Medusa's
level, or the castle, or Ludios.

Be willing to go back and clear out all the vaults once you get a
pickaxe; at the very least you can use that $ for protection.

Accumulate "bail-out" strategies with a suspenders-and-belt
philosophy--keep healing potions, and sleep wands, and teleport wands
& scrolls, etc in your main inventory.  Save them all for when they
are needed.

Polypile like mad, but carefully (plot out little piles to polymorph)

If you get in a situation where "I can probably win", run away! 
Remember that you need to be the overwhelming favorite in general to
win as much as you have to to ascend!

If you clear Gehennom, remember that in all likelihood the Planes are
WAY more dangerous than any situation you've been in since the Quest.



It's like playing blackjack with a single deck; if you count cards and
play smart, you can put the odds in your favor.  But gamble even once
in a while, and you're going to lose.

